WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.017
<v Instructor>Okay, so I am in the CloudWatch console,</v>

2
00:00:02.017 --> 00:00:04.204
and I'm gonna go on the left hand side for logs,

3
00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:08.790
and on the log management I will find all my log groups.

4
00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.169
So this is the logs that get created within AWS,

5
00:00:11.169 --> 00:00:13.740
and when it's a service creating a log,

6
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.690
you will see an AWS glue or a /Kinesis /Lambda,

7
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.222
which indicates it's a service log.

8
00:00:20.222 --> 00:00:23.040
But if you wanted to, you could also create

9
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.830
your own log groups by clicking on create log group,

10
00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.840
and you could enter whatever name you want.

11
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.360
For example, demo logs.

12
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.070
You could set up a retention policy

13
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.280
for this log standard in terms of storage class,

14
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.930
standard, or infrequent access,

15
00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.996
a KMS key if you wanted to encrypt things,

16
00:00:38.996 --> 00:00:41.515
as well as deviation protection if you wanted to prevent

17
00:00:41.515 --> 00:00:44.444
against accidental or unintended deletes,

18
00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:46.545
and then you would create your log group,

19
00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:48.989
and here you would find your log group as well.

20
00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:51.000
So then your application,

21
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.430
we need to send directly logs into your log group.

22
00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.639
So different options, but we have logs also created by AWS,

23
00:00:56.639 --> 00:00:58.020
and you may have different ones,

24
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.141
of course based on how you've been using this course.

25
00:01:00.141 --> 00:01:02.468
So here we have, for example,

26
00:01:02.468 --> 00:01:05.823
a function named HelloWorld,

27
00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.140
and so this function, HelloWorld, is a Lambda function

28
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.650
and that's what's going to actually populate logs in it.

29
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.270
So I have a HelloWorld function.

30
00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.890
If you don't have it, don't stress.

31
00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.993
You can just create a function, use a blueprint,

32
00:01:19.993 --> 00:01:24.300
and then you use the HelloWorld blueprint for Python.

33
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.783
You name your function HelloWorld and you're good to go,

34
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.550
and you would be good to go.

35
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.630
Okay, but I already have my function so here it is.

36
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.090
So the idea is that this function, when it runs,

37
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.400
it creates outputs, and this output gets sent

38
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.440
directly into CloudWatch logs, and it's the best way for us

39
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.055
to visualize how CloudWatch logs is working.

40
00:01:43.055 --> 00:01:46.410
So we scroll down and we have a look at log streams,

41
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.900
and log streams will be created over time.

42
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.344
It represents the different invocations

43
00:01:50.344 --> 00:01:52.408
of your Lambda instances in this instance,

44
00:01:52.408 --> 00:01:54.527
but log streams can be whatever you want

45
00:01:54.527 --> 00:01:56.760
and whatever the service wanted to be.

46
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.050
So you'll have different log streams

47
00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.020
and each log stream will just have logs inside.

48
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.058
So this is the logs from this first log stream,

49
00:02:03.058 --> 00:02:06.630
and then this will be the log from the second log stream.

50
00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.270
So you can see within each log stream,

51
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.670
we have a timestamp of when the data was created,

52
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.980
as well as the message which is corresponding

53
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.540
to the log line value.

54
00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.100
So everything that gets sent

55
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.815
from Lambda into CloudWatch logs is available here.

56
00:02:20.815 --> 00:02:23.220
So you can have a look, and this would be a very nice way

57
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.565
for you to debug your applications

58
00:02:24.565 --> 00:02:28.875
if you want to have this kind of capability

59
00:02:28.875 --> 00:02:30.135
when you have an error.

60
00:02:30.135 --> 00:02:32.167
So what can we do with these logs?

61
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.920
Well, you can explore them of course,

62
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.740
but you can also create what's called a metric filter.

63
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.835
So a metric filter will allow us

64
00:02:39.835 --> 00:02:42.865
to have a look at specific occurrences

65
00:02:42.865 --> 00:02:46.800
of certain texts or patterns in our log lines,

66
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.305
and we create a metric out of it.

67
00:02:48.305 --> 00:02:50.059
This is great for alarms.

68
00:02:50.059 --> 00:02:52.359
So here we can enter a filter

69
00:02:52.359 --> 00:02:54.780
and the filter can be quite complicated.

70
00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.753
You have a specific syntax that you don't have to learn,

71
00:02:57.753 --> 00:03:00.472
but just to mix in things very simple,

72
00:03:00.472 --> 00:03:05.290
I will just enter info here and let's see if that works,

73
00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:09.240
and we have our custom log data that's available here

74
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.160
that is provided by AWS.

75
00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.280
You test the pattern and it says, well, for the word info,

76
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.798
I found two log lines, and so this was created metrics

77
00:03:16.798 --> 00:03:18.867
for these two log lines.

78
00:03:18.867 --> 00:03:21.600
Of course you could also choose a log stream.

79
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.830
For example, this log stream,

80
00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.150
which is the log from our function,

81
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.270
and then we could choose, for example, the start keyword

82
00:03:30.241 --> 00:03:32.520
and then test it,

83
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.830
and we can see that we have different starts.

84
00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.960
And so it's interesting because maybe we'll say

85
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.620
that anytime there's a start and then the request ID,

86
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.870
we have a start of our Lambda function.

87
00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.531
Of course this is a metric already available to us

88
00:03:44.531 --> 00:03:47.520
in our Lambda function directly,

89
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.220
but we can recreate it using a metric filter.

90
00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.570
So as you can see in its start

91
00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:53.715
is going to trigger a false positive so it's up to you

92
00:03:53.715 --> 00:03:57.599
to really edit your filter pattern

93
00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:00.270
and maybe add better things

94
00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.220
so that you only have start request ID,

95
00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.450
and we're good to go.

96
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.340
All right, so this is for you to build.

97
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.759
This is just an interesting thing to do.

98
00:04:06.759 --> 00:04:11.193
So I call this one demo filter Lambda.

99
00:04:12.383 --> 00:04:16.560
I scroll down and then we have to enter a namespace.

100
00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.210
This is where your metric is going to be stored.

101
00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:19.361
So demo namespace.

102
00:04:19.361 --> 00:04:24.361
Metric name again, Countstart Lambda.

103
00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:25.980
Metric value.

104
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.470
So what happens when an occurrence is found?

105
00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.990
So we'll just have one to have account of one

106
00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:35.983
anytime we have a match, and then we can also add dimensions

107
00:04:35.983 --> 00:04:39.006
in case you have multiple accounts, multiple regions,

108
00:04:39.006 --> 00:04:41.926
or you're an organization, this is very helpful

109
00:04:41.926 --> 00:04:45.810
to have this kind of dimensions within your metrics.

110
00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.630
Click on next, we create this metric filter.

111
00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:52.896
So it has been created, and now this filter right here

112
00:04:52.896 --> 00:04:55.780
if I click on it, it's available to me here

113
00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:59.147
and so I can just now directly click on this,

114
00:04:59.147 --> 00:05:02.728
and it's gonna give me a link directly into my metric.

115
00:05:02.728 --> 00:05:05.342
So as you can see, my CloudWatch graph is empty

116
00:05:05.342 --> 00:05:09.540
because this metric is only going to get populated

117
00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.864
for the new values and not the historical values.

118
00:05:12.864 --> 00:05:15.720
So what I need to do to just demo you how this works

119
00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.210
is that I go into my function, I click on test,

120
00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.391
and I just click on test.

121
00:05:19.391 --> 00:05:21.226
I'll do it once or twice.

122
00:05:21.226 --> 00:05:23.237
Okay, I've done it four times,

123
00:05:23.237 --> 00:05:25.379
and now I just need to wait a little bit

124
00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:29.548
for CloudWatch to populate this metric.

125
00:05:29.548 --> 00:05:33.039
And so here in my metric, I had to select it

126
00:05:33.039 --> 00:05:34.800
and go back to count metric.

127
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.872
Here in my graph I see there's one data point

128
00:05:36.872 --> 00:05:39.122
for my metric Countstart Lambda.

129
00:05:39.122 --> 00:05:41.250
So it's not very fascinating as you can see,

130
00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.800
but we can see that this metric filter

131
00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.295
get populated from the logs,

132
00:05:45.295 --> 00:05:47.820
and what you can do on top of this now

133
00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.546
is that once you have a metric like this one,

134
00:05:50.546 --> 00:05:55.546
so you would go back into your CloudWatch log management.

135
00:05:56.285 --> 00:06:01.285
Let's take again our HelloWorld and then our metric filter.

136
00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.890
You can take this metric

137
00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.945
and then you would create an alarm on top of it,

138
00:06:06.945 --> 00:06:09.435
and this is cool because now you can get alerting

139
00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:13.500
based on the value of this metric so now it's a seven.

140
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.601
You can get an alarm based on this metric,

141
00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:17.922
and so you can say for example,

142
00:06:17.922 --> 00:06:20.621
well, whenever you have greater than 10,

143
00:06:20.621 --> 00:06:23.670
then you've started Lambda too many times in five minutes

144
00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.290
so I want an alarm on this.

145
00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:26.850
And this is very helpful because this is an alarm

146
00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.063
created on top of CloudWatch logs through a metric.

147
00:06:31.542 --> 00:06:34.887
So there are other things you can do in CloudWatch logs.

148
00:06:34.887 --> 00:06:37.530
For example, we can click on action here

149
00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.468
and we can edit the retention settings

150
00:06:39.468 --> 00:06:43.050
to say after how long the log will expire.

151
00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.860
We can also export the data to Amazon S3 directly

152
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.860
to have a data dump into Amazon S3,

153
00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.510
and we could also start tailing the logs.

154
00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:54.067
That means you can see the logs as they happen in real time.

155
00:06:54.067 --> 00:06:57.118
We also have the option to have subscription filters.

156
00:06:57.118 --> 00:06:59.210
This is a way for you to stream the logs

157
00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.290
into a different service.

158
00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:03.753
Here we have the option to use Amazon open search service.

159
00:07:03.753 --> 00:07:07.235
Kinesis, Amazon Data Firehose, or Lambda.

160
00:07:07.235 --> 00:07:09.630
These are the four destinations available right now.

161
00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.801
If there's more, this is fine. (chuckles)

162
00:07:11.801 --> 00:07:15.720
And then the last thing we can do

163
00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.720
is to have a look at CloudWatch logs insights.

164
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.738
So if I have a look at CloudWatch logs insights,

165
00:07:20.738 --> 00:07:24.238
here we have the option to query our logs

166
00:07:24.238 --> 00:07:29.238
using a query language, and it's called Logs Insight QL.

167
00:07:30.239 --> 00:07:33.781
So you would enter a query, scroll down,

168
00:07:33.781 --> 00:07:36.577
and then you would click on run query.

169
00:07:36.577 --> 00:07:39.892
And this is going to run the query, and as you can see,

170
00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:42.275
we get the results of this query right here,

171
00:07:42.275 --> 00:07:44.571
and we have 70 lines of logs.

172
00:07:44.571 --> 00:07:47.635
We can get a graph of how the log is over time,

173
00:07:47.635 --> 00:07:50.010
and we can get the results of this query.

174
00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:51.625
Now, this is just an example,

175
00:07:51.625 --> 00:07:54.450
but this can be very complicated

176
00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.540
in terms of the types of query you make of course.

177
00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.980
For example, you can get some magic ideas

178
00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.260
by using these facets and fields,

179
00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.410
and this is based from your log or look up tables.

180
00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.810
But if you want to have pre-made queries as well,

181
00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:10.995
you could click on this little folder,

182
00:08:10.995 --> 00:08:12.900
and it shows you the saved queries.

183
00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:14.817
This is also accessible from the top

184
00:08:14.817 --> 00:08:16.878
and you could have common queries, for example,

185
00:08:16.878 --> 00:08:19.350
the 25 most recently added log events,

186
00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:23.400
but also the number of exceptions logged every five minutes,

187
00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.666
or for example, for some specific services,

188
00:08:25.666 --> 00:08:28.145
the latency statistics for five minute intervals,

189
00:08:28.145 --> 00:08:31.740
and you can just learn, you know, there's max, mins,

190
00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.990
average, binning every five minutes.

191
00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:35.580
So lots of different things you can do

192
00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:37.080
with CloudWatch logs inside.

193
00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.230
Again, just for you to know, for real world practice.

194
00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:41.400
Not necessarily for the exam.

195
00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.965
You want to know how to read this or type it.

196
00:08:43.965 --> 00:08:45.857
So that's it for CloudWatch logs.

197
00:08:45.857 --> 00:08:49.833
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

